<p>&quot;One need not worry about the monster behind the seal; it is quite secure in that nebulous other-world, and can not affect us while the seals are closed. No, dear friend, fear not the monster behind the seal; fear the human who wishes to break the seal.&quot;</p>
<p>And I disagree with BlackJack completely, that actually DOES seem like the type of &quot;Well that's atypical&quot; nonsense which <em>would</em> attract someone from the Foundation's attention. Especially since you directly stated the fact that it wasn't enough to draw full action at the start, just enough for him to keep an eye on it.</p>
<p>And, are the effects cliche? Maybe. But the work, that's why they were done. A player who was particularly apathetic towards his characters and was willing to sacrifice any number of them to accomplish his task wouldn't run into endless death-waves with a narrow opening; he'd be boxed in to a point that progress was literally impossible. Meanwhile, a player who did everything to make sure everyone was OK and equally advancing would find their group constantly split apart, taken out one by one. The whole reason the alter was used was because something like that was what tweaked *that* player in another session, so the AI (or worse) went &quot;Well if you thought THAT was bad&#8230;&quot;</p>

